Parse chunks by their bytes, the automatic type directives' files first, large files largest first

The checkers start when every file is parsed. Once the largest file no longer decides when that is
(the next change), the other files do, and on the TypeScript compiler project they took 35 ms from
the moment the root files were queued, for 25 ms of work on nine threads:

- The 78 root files went out in chunks of sixteen files, 2.4 MB each: four chunks on three threads,
  one of them queued behind another, 25 ms each, while six threads had nothing left. A chunk is now
  also bounded by its bytes (192 KB, about 3 ms of a parse thread).
- The automatic type directives were one job in the first chunk of root files and came back with it
  29 ms later, with the 65 files of @types/node to queue, and those with undici-types: two more
  rounds at the end. They go out with the libraries, and so does the entry file they add.
- While root files are matched, chunks are queued on the threads ahead, twelve each: for a match of
  a millisecond that was the whole queue on whichever threads were idle. A thread now holds as many
  bytes ahead as it loads in the time the match has taken so far, the first files of a match wait
  2 ms for the others, and the large files go out the largest first.

The last chunk other than checker.ts's came back at 49.3 ms (minimum) and 54.4 (median) of fifteen
runs before, at 41.5 and 50.4 with this on a loaded machine; the order files are loaded ahead in
changes nothing the program is built from.

